    bio: "Franz Boas was born in Minden, Westphalia, in 1858, into a Jewish family of liberal, freethinking outlook. He studied physics and geography at German universities and completed a doctorate on the optical properties of water — an unlikely beginning for the man who would eventually be called the Father of American Anthropology. His intellectual journey toward the study of human cultures began with a geographical expedition to Baffin Island in 1883, where he spent a year living among the Inuit and came away with a conviction that would define the rest of his life: that no culture could be understood except on its own terms, and that no people were more or less 'advanced' than any other.\n\nBoas emigrated to the United States in 1887 and eventually joined Columbia University, where he taught for four decades and trained virtually an entire generation of American anthropologists — including Margaret Mead, Ruth Benedict, and Zora Neale Hurston. He pioneered the concept of cultural relativism: the principle that human cultures must be understood from within, not ranked according to a single evolutionary ladder with Western civilisation perched at the top. This was not a comfortable idea in the late nineteenth century, and Boas spent much of his career in open combat with the racial determinists and craniometrists who dominated public discourse about human difference.\n\nHis 1912 study of the skull shapes of immigrants — demonstrating that cranial form changed measurably within a single generation in response to environmental conditions — was a direct and deliberate attack on the scientific foundations of craniometry. It showed that the physical measurements used to define and rank races were not fixed biological facts but malleable responses to circumstance. The study was so threatening to established hierarchies that it attracted fierce counter-attacks, and a controversy about its methodology still rumbles in specialist journals more than a century later — though the broad thrust of his findings has been repeatedly upheld.\n\nBoas was a tireless and sometimes combative public intellectual who spoke out against Nazi racial ideology when it was still possible to imagine doing so politely. He helped establish the American Anthropological Association\'s formal rejection of scientific racism and continued publishing and lecturing into his eighties. He died in December 1942, at a dinner in his honour, mid-conversation — apparently mid-argument, which those who knew him found entirely fitting. His insistence that human beings are shaped by culture, history, and environment rather than by biology alone remains the foundation on which modern anthropology stands."
